    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d429">

                <pb facs="0551" n="541" xml:id="pg541"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/7644: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-10-24T09:00:00+07:00">October 24, 1942—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:09 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1216. From Adler for Secretary of the Treasury.</p>
                <p>TF–64. Weekly Economic.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>2. Dr. Kung has asked me informally to raise question of Chinese Government
                    purchasing United States dollars 20 million of gold from United States Treasury
                    with funds from the American loan. Such purchase is contemplated, subject to
                    Treasury, consent independently of whether or not scheme for a free gold market
                    mentioned in 2 and 3 (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) of TF–63 of October
                        8<anchor n="89" xml:id="d429-f1"/> is adopted. With respect to this scheme
                    Dr. Kung informed me Generalissimo has still not consented to it on ground that
                    some of the gold may reach enemy hands. [Morgenthau.]</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
